# Self-reference

**Self-reference** is the property of a statement, program or system that
refers to itself, contains a representation of itself, or takes itself as
input. It is the broad family to which the [[strange-loop]] belongs. The
discussion's position is that the family is large, that its members work by
different mechanisms, and that most of them are not strange loops.

## Description

The starting observation was that mathematics is full of self-reference:
the factorial function and every recursive sequence, Gödel's proof, the
constructible universe, programs that print their own source. The proposal
was to stop looking for one definition that covers all of them and to treat
mathematical self-reference as a class to be sorted. The sorting is the
[[taxonomy-of-self-reference]], with eight classes from recursive generation
to self-modelling cognition.

Sorting shows that "refers to itself" covers at least four different things.

| What is meant | Example | Is anything referred to? |
|---|---|---|
| A rule applied to its own output | factorial, a fractal | No. A definition mentions itself; nothing is said about it |
| A description that reproduces itself | a [[quine]] | The source is reproduced, not reasoned about |
| A statement about its own code | the [[godel-sentence]] | Yes, through an encoding |
| A system that models itself | a reflective agent, a mind | Yes, through a model that takes part in what it models |

Three distinctions from the discussion keep these apart.

- **Self-similarity is not self-reference.** A fractal resembles itself at
  other scales. It does not, by that fact, assert anything about the rules of
  its own description. This is the subject of [[self-similarity]].
- **Duality is not self-reference.** A vector space and its dual, a set and
  its complement, a Boolean algebra and its Stone space reconstruct each
  other. Two structures that determine one another are still two structures,
  and no loop is produced.
- **Reproducing is not reasoning.** A quine reproduces a description; a
  reflective interpreter can reason about a description. Neither inherently
  implies incompleteness.

A fourth distinction concerns what self-reference yields. The *existence* of
self-reference is one thing, and *a theorem about its consequences* is
another. Gödel's construction has both: a sentence that refers to itself, and
a proof that the theory cannot decide it. A quine has only the first.

Self-reference turns paradoxical when it is semantic and unrestricted. The
[[liar-paradox]], [[russell-paradox]] and [[berry-paradox]] form their own
class in the taxonomy. Gödel's sentence is the liar with truth replaced by
provability, and the replacement is what makes it a theorem instead of a
contradiction.

### Where the strange loop sits

The working formulation reached at the end of the discussion defines a
strange loop as "a self-referential structure arising through representational
level crossing". Self-reference is the genus. The crossing of levels is the
difference. A recursive function is self-referential in the weak first sense
and crosses nothing.

## Relations

- A [[strange-loop]] {relation: generalizes} is self-reference that arises through a crossing of levels.
- It is sorted into eight classes by the [[taxonomy-of-self-reference]] {relation: part-of}.
- It is not [[self-similarity]] {relation: distinguished-from}: resemblance across scales asserts nothing about itself.
- It is not [[duality]] {relation: distinguished-from}: mutual reconstruction of two structures is not one structure referring to itself.
- [[recursion]] {relation: example-of} is its weakest form: a definition that mentions itself.
- A [[quine]] {relation: example-of} is its reproductive form: a program whose output is its own source.
- The [[godel-sentence]] {relation: example-of} is its exact formal form.
- [[diagonalization]] {relation: uses} is the standard method for producing it inside a formal system.
- A [[fixed-point]] {relation: analogous-to} is its mathematical shape: an object that a transformation returns unchanged.
- It bears on the question [[strange-loop-meaning]] {relation: answers}, as the family from which the concept has to be cut out.

## Neighbouring concepts

- **[[taxonomy-of-self-reference]].** Eight overlapping classes, each with its
  own mechanism, offered in place of a forced universal definition. It is the
  full sorting of what this card introduces.
- **[[strange-loop]].** A return to the starting level through a hierarchy.
  It is the member of this family that Hofstadter singled out.
- **[[self-similarity]].** Repetition of a pattern across scales. It is the
  concept most easily mistaken for self-reference, and involves no reference.
- **[[godel-sentence]].** A sentence provably equivalent to the statement that
  it is not provable. It is self-reference with every step written out.
- **[[quine]].** A program that outputs its own source text. It shows that
  self-reference can be achieved with no paradox and no limitation.

